1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,105
Good evening from the OROS closing desk.

2
00:00:03,105 --> 00:00:09,005
This morning the system named no pick, so tonight there is nothing to grade.

3
00:00:09,005 --> 00:00:13,740
No setup, no trade, no loss — that is the discipline working.

4
00:00:13,740 --> 00:00:18,676
Why we grade in public: a forecast that is never checked is just content.

5
00:00:18,676 --> 00:00:24,085
Every morning call gets an evening verdict, and both go in the permanent record.

6
00:00:24,050 --> 00:00:27,922
On the day, the book is up 373 dollars.

7
00:00:27,922 --> 00:00:32,490
Digi International (DGII) leads, +2.0 percent.

8
00:00:32,490 --> 00:00:38,149
First Trust NASDAQ Cybersecurity ETF (CIBR) lags at -2.2.

9
00:00:38,149 --> 00:00:43,511
The checklist is clear: nothing requires action today.

10
00:00:43,511 --> 00:00:47,038
On screen, the day's gainers and laggards inside the paper book.

11
00:00:46,994 --> 00:00:55,144
And here is the book itself — the actual holdings, identical to this morning's emailed report.

12
00:00:55,144 --> 00:01:07,281
11 positions, led by Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing, ticker TSM, First Trust NASDAQ Cybersecurity ETF, ticker CIBR, AbbVie, ticker ABBV.

13
00:01:07,281 --> 00:01:19,072
Every line carries its share count, its entry, and its exit — because a position without a defined exit is not a position, it is a hope.

14
00:01:19,037 --> 00:01:23,544
Now the part that makes this system different: the loop.

15
00:01:23,544 --> 00:01:25,797
Every morning OROS proposes.

16
00:01:25,797 --> 00:01:35,696
Every evening it grades its own call, journals the verdict, and adjusts its own risk before it is allowed to propose again.

17
00:01:35,696 --> 00:01:39,398
Inside the book today: 7 positions up, 4 down.

18
00:01:39,398 --> 00:01:41,651
Best, DGII at +2.02 percent.

19
00:01:41,651 --> 00:01:43,342
Worst, CIBR at -2.17.

20
00:01:43,342 --> 00:01:48,009
Both go in the journal — wins and losses get the same ink.

21
00:01:48,009 --> 00:01:54,769
The honest scoreboard reads: expectancy -3.00% per trade over 1003 graded positions.

22
00:01:54,769 --> 00:02:01,369
Because that record is soft, the system has throttled itself to 50% position size.

23
00:02:01,369 --> 00:02:06,197
Nobody told it to — full size must be earned back with wins.

24
00:02:06,197 --> 00:02:17,786
What tonight's read hands to tomorrow's open: 189 names close the day in Stage 2; position size stays cut to 50% until the record earns it back.

25
00:02:17,737 --> 00:02:19,871
Over to the sports desk.

26
00:02:19,871 --> 00:02:28,676
Day 60 of the race: three books, the same one hundred thousand simulated dollars, since July sixth.

27
00:02:28,676 --> 00:02:33,301
In first place, the index fund, in gray, at 102,926.

28
00:02:33,301 --> 00:02:39,883
Then the live self-rebuilding book, in blue, 1.9 thousand back at 101,070.

29
00:02:39,883 --> 00:02:46,731
Then the frozen first portfolio, the green line, 5.2 thousand back at 97,705.

30
00:02:46,731 --> 00:02:49,221
The race has no finish line.

31
00:02:49,221 --> 00:02:53,312
Same books, same dollars, back at it tomorrow.

32
00:02:53,312 --> 00:02:57,937
Every line starts the same day with the same dollar.

33
00:02:57,937 --> 00:02:59,716
Nothing is restated.

34
00:02:59,716 --> 00:03:02,045
Today's lesson is benchmarking.

35
00:03:02,045 --> 00:03:08,507
Any strategy must beat the boring alternative: the same money parked in an index fund.

36
00:03:08,507 --> 00:03:11,963
That is why the S and P line shares the chart.

37
00:03:11,963 --> 00:03:20,979
If the system cannot outrun it, the index deserves the money, and publishing the race every day keeps the system honest.

38
00:03:20,939 --> 00:03:28,274
All figures are simulated paper trading, graded and journaled by the OROS agents with no human intervention.

39
00:03:28,274 --> 00:03:30,515
Nothing here is financial advice.

40
00:03:30,515 --> 00:03:33,504
From the closing desk — see you at the open.
